'''Muna Xasan''' (born 11 May 1984) is a Burawi politician and journalist who has served as the [[Member of the Lower House of Burawa|Member of the Lower House]] for [[Cadina-1]] since 2018. A member of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]] (PEP), she is the party's candidate for [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in the [[2026 Burawi snap election]]. If elected, she would become the first female Leader in Burawi history.

Xasan is regarded as the second most influential figure within the Party for Equitable Progress, behind the party leader. She is known for her legislative work on healthcare and labour rights, including authorship of the [[Healthcare Expansion Act of 2022]] and the [[Industrial Labour Transition Act of 2023]].

== Early life and education ==

Muna Xasan was born on 11 May 1984 in [[Iskhal]], a city in southwestern Burawa. She completed her secondary education in Iskhal before entering the [[Burawi Work Programme]] track in the [[Burawi Armed Forces|military]], as required for all Burawi citizens.

Following her military service, Xasan enrolled at the [[People's University of Kankadadka]], initially studying chemistry. She later changed her major to socialist political theory. During her university years, she became active in feminist activism and participated in the 2004 women's protests advocating for optional temporary leave for women during menstruation. The protests led to the passage of the [[Women's Health Protection Act of 2005]], which allowed women to obtain medical leave for severe menstrual symptoms with physician certification.

== Early career ==

=== Journalism and community politics ===

After graduating from university, Xasan worked as a political organizer and investigative journalist for the ''[[Iskhal Times]]'' newspaper. Her reporting focused on local government accountability and social justice issues.

From 2010 to 2014, she served as a community council member for Iskhal-2-14, a neighbourhood within the city of Iskhal. Her work on the council focused on housing policy and community health services.

=== Political organizing ===

In 2014, Xasan relocated to [[Cadina]] to work as a political organizer for the Party for Equitable Progress. She served as an assistant on several PEP electoral campaigns, developing expertise in grassroots mobilisation and voter outreach.

== Parliamentary career ==

=== 2016 election ===

Xasan first contested the Cadina-1 seat in the [[2016 Burawi Lower House election]], losing to the incumbent. Following the defeat, she continued her work as a political organizer and built support within the constituency.

=== 2018 election and first term ===

In the [[2018 Burawi Lower House election]], Xasan won the Cadina-1 seat, beginning her first term in the Lower House. She aligned herself with the progressive wing of the PEP and focused on healthcare and labour policy.

=== Healthcare Expansion Act of 2022 ===

{{main|Healthcare Expansion Act of 2022}}

Xasan was the principal author of the [[Healthcare Expansion Act of 2022]], which expanded state healthcare coverage to include subsidised transition-related medication for transgender citizens. The legislation, which passed with support from the Socialist Party, the Green Party, and the Communist Party, was regarded as a significant advancement in LGBTQ+ rights in Burawa.

The act generated controversy among conservative and religious members of parliament, with the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] opposing the measure. Xasan defended the legislation as consistent with the constitutional guarantee of healthcare without discrimination.

=== Industrial Labour Transition Act of 2023 ===

{{main|Industrial Labour Transition Act of 2023}}

Xasan also authored the [[Industrial Labour Transition Act of 2023]], which reduced the pension eligibility age for workers in the mining and petroleum industries from 65 to 60. The legislation recognised the physically demanding nature of extractive industry work and the shorter life expectancy of workers in these sectors.

The act passed with broad cross-party support, including from the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] and the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], both of which drew support from regions with significant mining and petroleum activity.

=== Subsequent terms ===

Xasan was re-elected to the Cadina-1 seat in the [[2020 Burawi Lower House election|2020]], [[2022 Burawi Lower House election|2022]], and [[2024 Burawi Lower House election|2024]] elections. She is currently serving her fourth term as of 2026.

=== Rise within the Party for Equitable Progress ===

Xasan rose to become the second most influential figure within the Party for Equitable Progress through her work as an active party member and organiser. Known as a skilled orator and effective campaigner, she developed a reputation for grassroots mobilisation by travelling to districts where PEP was competitive and conducting door-to-door voter outreach. Her ability to connect with voters in person contributed to her influence within the party and her electoral success in Cadina-1.

== 2026 leadership campaign ==

{{main|2026 Burawi snap election}}

On 25 March 2026, the Party for Equitable Progress announced that Xasan would serve as its candidate for [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in the [[2026 Burawi snap election]]. The party, which had abstained from both recall votes against incumbent Leader [[Hancil Thea]], stated that its decision to nominate a candidate stemmed from opposition to the agreement between the Thea administration and the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] that had reduced corporate taxes from 30 per cent to 25 per cent. Party leaders characterised the agreement as a corrupt bargain that undermined the recall process and betrayed the progressive coalition's principles.

Xasan's campaign emphasises her experience in healthcare and labour policy, her record of legislative accomplishment, and her potential to become the first female Leader of Burawa. She has criticised both the recall process and the Industrial Party agreement, calling for a return to principle-based governance.

Her campaign platform focuses on four central themes:

* '''Opposition to the corporate tax deal''': Xasan has characterised the agreement between the Thea administration and the Industrial Party as a corrupt bargain that undermined the recall process, and has pledged to restore the previous corporate tax rate.
* '''Reform of the Forward Party''': Xasan has called for the Forward Party to undergo internal reform, arguing that the party's internal divisions and leadership struggles have weakened the progressive coalition.
* '''Progressive advancement''': Xasan has emphasised the need to keep Burawa "progressing to the future" with better access to services and equality for all citizens.
* '''Guarding against political malfeasance''': Xasan has warned against "bad political actors" who prioritise personal gain over the public interest, positioning herself as a principled alternative to the deal-making that characterised the recall process.

=== Party endorsements ===

On 3 April 2026, [[Faarax Mikael]] of the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]] announced his withdrawal from the leadership race and endorsed Xasan. Mikael, who had received 3 per cent of first-preference votes in the [[2025 Burawi leadership election|2025 election]], cited the need to consolidate the progressive vote and prevent a split that could benefit Thea's re-election bid.

On 26 March 2026, the [[Forward Progressive Party]] — a breakaway faction of the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] led by [[Mahad Allaale]] — announced it would not field its own candidate and instead endorsed Xasan. The endorsement followed the Forward Party split triggered by [[Marik Sahamin]]'s leadership candidacy announcement, and aligned the Forward Progressive Party's 77 Senate seats and 60 Lower House seats with Xasan's campaign.

=== Debates ===

Xasan participated in the first televised debate on 8 April 2026, alongside all six candidates: [[Hancil Thea]], [[Xasan Garaad]], [[Marik Sahamin]], [[Liban Qaasim]], and [[Giorgis Nuur]]. The debate, moderated by the [[Burawi Broadcasting Corporation]], focused on constitutional legitimacy, economic policy, and regional development. Analysts widely assessed Xasan as the debate's winner, citing her articulate critique of the Industrial Party agreement and her appeal to both progressive and moderate voters.

A second debate was held on 12 April 2026, addressing healthcare reform, infrastructure investment, and foreign policy. Xasan's performance reinforced her frontrunner status, while Garaad and Thea engaged in heated exchanges over the recall's legitimacy.

=== Polling surge ===

Following the first debate, Xasan experienced a significant surge in opinion polls. A poll conducted on 7 April showed Xasan at 18 per cent, trailing [[Xasan Garaad]] (22 per cent) and [[Hancil Thea]] (20 per cent). By 9 April, following the first debate, support for Xasan had risen to 35 per cent, making her the clear frontrunner. A poll on 12 April recorded Xasan at 38 per cent, maintaining her lead over Thea (22 per cent) and Garaad (16 per cent).

=== Potential historic significance ===

If elected, Xasan would become the first female [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in the country's history. Women have served as [[President of Burawa|President]] and [[Prime Minister of Burawa|Prime Minister]], but the Leadership has been held exclusively by men since the establishment of the republic.

== Political positions ==

Xasan is associated with the progressive wing of Burawi politics. Her legislative record demonstrates support for:

* '''Healthcare expansion''': Advocates for comprehensive state healthcare coverage, including gender-affirming care.
* '''Labour rights''': Supports improved working conditions and early retirement for workers in physically demanding industries.
* '''Women's rights''': Has a long record of feminist activism and advocacy for women's health policies.
* '''LGBTQ+ rights''': Authored legislation expanding healthcare access for transgender citizens.
* '''Progressive taxation''': Opposed the Industrial Party agreement reducing corporate taxes.

== Personal life ==

Xasan is married to Cabdullahi Warsame. The couple has one child and resides in [[Cadina]]. She is irreligious.

